perceive your leadership behavior is

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:41.490
what's super interesting to me.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.450
And I think the last thing
is being international,

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:49.000
I wanted to go to a school that
had some brand recognition in my

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.580
home market since I know that I'm planning
to go back there after graduation.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:56.730
Okay, next question.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:02.640
Carl, how was your experience in the
beginning of the MBA course considering

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.500
that you did not have
a traditional business background?

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:09.325
How did the school help you
during this transition period?

00:07:09.325 --> 00:07:12.030
I think there's a few different things.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:16.340
First having a non-traditional
background in the first few

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.670
quarters a lot of the classes are tiered.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:22.810
So there's a base level and
accelerated level and an advanced level.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:27.770
And so for
me who hadn't taken accounting before knew

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:32.190
very little about finance, it was helpful
to take the base classes in some cases

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:34.610
accelerated is also appropriate.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:38.640
Whereas my classmates who came from
finance or consulting backgrounds

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:42.470
were probably skewed more towards
the accelerated and advanced classes.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:47.677
Also, we got sent materials over
the summer that we could look over

00:07:47.677 --> 00:07:51.967
if we needed to review certain things,
mainly in math and

00:07:51.967 --> 00:07:55.167
statistics, which I
looked into a little bit.

00:07:56.277 --> 00:08:00.544
And there's also a lot of tutoring
resources that are available for us.

00:08:00.544 --> 00:08:04.803
To access all throughout
our two years in campus.

00:08:04.803 --> 00:08:08.000
&gt;&gt; And I think one of the other
interesting things to note there that is

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.660
the GSB specific thing is we have
an academic adjustment period for

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:14.290
the first six weeks of your first year.

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.336
You're not allowed to join the clubs,

00:08:16.336 --> 00:08:20.552
recruiters aren't allowed to come to
campus, and those six weeks are purely for

00:08:20.552 --> 00:08:24.401
you to kind of focus on getting your
feet underneath your academics life.

00:08:24.401 --> 00:08:26.701
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, yeah,
that was definitely useful for

00:08:26.701 --> 00:08:29.300
me as someone who's never
studied business before.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.610
Next question, Heather, can you speak
a little bit more about the social impact

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:38.355
opportunities at Stanford and unique
programs this school has in the space?

00:08:38.355 --> 00:08:43.230
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, sure, so
we have a one of the biggest resources

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:45.490
here on campus is the Center for
Social Innovation.

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:51.040
They are sort of a conduit or
a plethora of resources for

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:58.120
you in career preparation,
as well as plotting your academic journey.

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:01.686
So when you come to school, they sort
of talk to you about your two year

00:09:01.686 --> 00:09:05.641
roadmap and then beyond that, what does
it look like once you leave the GSB.

00:09:05.641 --> 00:09:08.540
They bucket you into one of
I think four categories, so

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:12.068
you might be a career switcher, or
you might be an industry vet, or

00:09:12.068 --> 00:09:15.737
you might just be an explorer,
just kind of interested in the field.

00:09:15.737 --> 00:09:19.750
So based on that they kind of help
give you guidance on on what to do.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:23.320
I think one of the other great resources
that we have available is you can actually

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:29.240
get a certificate here, a certificate in
social innovation and public management.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:33.260
And we're generalizing BA program,
we don't do specific tracks,

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:37.240
that's one of our only
certificate programs.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:41.758
So I think that's been a really great
way to sort of add credentials to my MBA

00:09:41.758 --> 00:09:44.213
showing that this isn't area of focus.

00:09:44.213 --> 00:09:50.320
And then I joined a lot of social impact
type opportunity in terms of clubs.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.860
So we have a club for
sustainable business, we have a club for

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.360
social innovation, I joined our

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:04.710
social impact start up that we would
invest in, and then I am the CEO

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.350
of the Challenge for Charity which is
the largest MBA nonprofit in the country.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:14.080
So they were things that were important
to me I found ways to work them into,

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.570
none of my course work but
my extra curriculars,

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:21.270
and just other opportunity,
yeah, my internship.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:26.455
&gt;&gt; Amazing, GSB focuses a lot on shaping
leaders, what kind of leaders and

00:10:26.455 --> 00:10:30.880
what are the leadership values
that form the ethos of the GSB?

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.274
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm, do you want to start?

00:10:32.274 --> 00:10:36.995
&gt;&gt; Sure, I'll start, I think a big thing
that I've learned during my time here

00:10:36.995 --> 00:10:41.160
is that there's not one kind of
leader that can be effective.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.040
There's so many people who
are extremely different than me here,

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:50.000
and they're just as effective as me if not
more in a lot of different situations.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.100
And I think a big strength in
the GP is that they help you narrow

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:58.860
in on what your brand is, and
what situation is this brand effective,

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:04.080
and what situation could you tweak it, in
a way that still feels authentic to you.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:09.240
And I think in terms of what they look
for, passion is certainly something that

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.760
resonated a lot with me when
I was visiting the school,

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:14.330
when I was talking to the students.

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:18.280
I think it's not a coincidence that the
question in the essay is what matters most

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:19.240
to you and why.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:22.538
They really want to get at
what you're passionate about,

00:11:22.538 --> 00:11:25.786
what drives you,
what gets you out of bed in the morning.

00:11:25.786 --> 00:11:29.935
&gt;&gt; I agree with all of that,
I think if you were to ask people,

00:11:29.935 --> 00:11:35.119
just ask them in general public like
what is the brand of GSB leadership?

00:11:35.119 --> 00:11:40.046
They would probably talk to you about how
we're sort of in touch with our feelings

00:11:40.046 --> 00:11:44.247
and our emotions, and I would say
that's definitely been a big focus

00:11:44.247 --> 00:11:46.878
intentionally of my first year and a half.

00:11:46.878 --> 00:11:51.614
Has really been doing this self-reflection
and identifying what I want to get out of

00:11:51.614 --> 00:11:55.147
being a leader or being in business,
what it is that drives me,

00:11:55.147 --> 00:11:59.570
what it is that I'm passionate about, and
how I want to show up to other people.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:02.755
And I think one of the most
valuable pieces of the GSB is that,

00:12:02.755 --> 00:12:07.090
there's just this assumption here that
everyone has the best intent possible, and

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:08.902
there is this culture of feedback.

00:12:08.902 --> 00:12:13.940
And it's really a gift to
other people to let them know

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.760
how you perceive them and
how they are showing up to you

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.700
because that is not always information
that you get in the real world.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:23.910
And so what you may intend to do
based on how you're speaking or

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.820
what you're doing,
may not be how other people perceive it.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:30.560
And so that's been a really valuable part
of this experience to me is understanding

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:33.450
how my intent differs from
the impact that I have on others.

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:38.010
And so
I think that that is a self aware leader,

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:41.850
I guess is where I think
that GSP tries to push us.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely agree with that.

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:48.545
Next question, what was your most
memorable experience at the GSB?

00:12:48.545 --> 00:12:50.610
want to start with that one?

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:53.487
&gt;&gt; Sure, wow, they're so many.

00:12:53.487 --> 00:12:56.244
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, my first challenge for

00:12:56.244 --> 00:12:58.729
