00:12:58.729 --> 00:13:03.935
was a really great culminating experience
of a lot of hard work throughout the year.

00:13:03.935 --> 00:13:10.123
And then also an opportunity to host other
business schools here which helped us or

00:13:10.123 --> 00:13:14.621
helped me see GSB through their eyes,
which is kind of fun.

00:13:14.621 --> 00:13:19.697
And I'm trying to think that I also
loved our Vegas foam, [LAUGH] so

00:13:19.697 --> 00:13:25.049
our foam is just a weekly social
event that we have on Tuesday nights,

00:13:25.049 --> 00:13:29.597
and it's just like a kind of
a happy hour type experience.

00:13:29.597 --> 00:13:31.863
And once a year,
we all dress up in 70s clothes and

00:13:31.863 --> 00:13:36.000
we take a cheap Southwest flight to Vegas
for the night, and then we come back.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.580
And that's not normally something I think
I would participate in at this stage in my

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:44.680
life but it is really fun to do
with 200 of your closest friends.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:48.130
So that was a very memorable experience,
and then otherwise,

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:49.860
I think just the friendships
that I've made and

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.550
the trips we've taken together,
that's been a big part.

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:55.120
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think for me also,

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:59.620
a lot of these travel opportunities
have really memorable for me.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:04.410
I actually met Heather before we even
got to campus on a trip to Singapore and

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:08.480
Malaysia that was organized In
the context of our program.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:11.380
And so we spend two weeks in
those countries learning about

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:14.260
their approach to artificial
intelligence and big data.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.340
And it was such a great opportunity for

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:19.220
me to meet some of my classmates
from different backgrounds.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:23.900
To get to know them in a bit
of a Smaller environment and

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:27.900
really feel like I had a lot of friendly
faces on campus when I already got there.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:31.070
And on top of that I got to learn about
a new industry learned about two new

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:33.680
countries experience their culture.

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:35.560
What was really exceptional?

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:36.480
&gt;&gt; This is a fun question.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:37.460
So can I add two more?

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:39.010
&gt;&gt; Yeah, go
&gt;&gt; really quickly.

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:41.780
So when Carl and I are very,
very good friends.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:45.010
I actually had the honor
of introducing Carl, for

00:14:45.010 --> 00:14:46.900
like a big event that he did on campus.

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:48.120
He gave a big speech.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:53.880
And so that was a really memorable I think
opportunity for us together and then For

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:58.860
me and another big piece,
because we're Arbuckle fellows,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:03.490
we had the opportunity to take the
interpersonal dynamics class last spring.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:07.449
And that's sort of a small group
type learning for Focused on, yeah,

00:15:07.449 --> 00:15:11.920
self-awareness, sometimes
called touchy-feely, and

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:16.320
that course had a huge impact on me
not only in a professional setting but

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.150
how I approach my relationships and

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:22.820
friendships and it has really impacted
a lot about how I live my life.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:23.880
So [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:25.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, those two were also really-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:27.370
&gt;&gt; Really impactful experiences for

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:28.020
me definitely.

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:34.500
Next can you speak more on the health care
focused opportunities, both at the GSP and

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:38.450
beyond, for example, classes, clubs,
exit opportunities, etcetera.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:40.555
I think I can take that one.

00:15:40.555 --> 00:15:42.520
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:46.020
&gt;&gt; I have taken a lot of healthcare
classes both at the GSP and

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:49.879
what we call across the street,
so in other Standford faculties.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:55.470
I've taken one on innovation in healthcare
where each class is about a different

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:57.460
segment of the healthcare industry.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:02.624
We had lots of speakers come in and CEOs
of Pharma companies, biotech companies,

00:16:02.624 --> 00:16:07.200
of drug discovery and medical device
companies, to talk to us about how they

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:11.790
perceive the industries, what
are the major trends within their segment.

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:15.862
And that was super interesting for
me to see health care from the sort of

00:16:15.862 --> 00:16:19.744
the business perspective rather
than the clinical perspective.

00:16:19.744 --> 00:16:24.778
I've also taken a health care
operations management class, where I've

00:16:24.778 --> 00:16:29.746
learned how to take large chunks of
data of hospital patient transfers.

00:16:29.746 --> 00:16:32.650
And then see in a consulting type

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:38.360
of project how we could optimize transfers
between the different departments.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:41.058
In terms of the club,
our health care club is very active.

00:16:41.058 --> 00:16:46.660
They always organize brown bag lunches
with different speakers from startups and

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:48.680
large companies alike.

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:53.060
They organized this week a trek to
a bunch of the startups operating in

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:55.866
the mental health space in Silicon Valley.

00:16:55.866 --> 00:17:00.545
And they're also organizing a large
conference later in the spring.

00:17:00.545 --> 00:17:03.950
And finally you were asking
about exit opportunities.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:09.072
I think there's people who do all sorts
of things within health care, whether

00:17:09.072 --> 00:17:14.450
health care investing, in VC, some people
will go work at health care startups.

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:17.140
Some people will do general
health care administration.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.880
Some people like me will work

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:25.670
as clinicians with leadership roles
within the health care system.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:30.240
And so, I think the world is kind of your
oyster within the health care industry

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:31.495
once you leave here.

00:17:34.187 --> 00:17:36.872
Is there anything you
wish you had known or

00:17:36.872 --> 00:17:39.874
done differently before
coming into the MBA?

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:48.029
&gt;&gt; Yes,
I think that the opportunities that you're

00:17:48.029 --> 00:17:51.550
presented at Stanford are numerous.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:54.690
There's always going to be
a lot of competing options for

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.700
your time and attention.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:18:00.530
At any given time, there's four brown
bag lunches that you want to go to, and

00:18:00.530 --> 00:18:01.750
they're all great.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:06.130
I just had a, was sending emails, and my
co-founder was like, I'm going to go see

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:10.110
this former founder, can you go talk
to the VC people in the other room?

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:15.880
And so it's a matter of making choices
about the opportunities that you take.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:20.930
And I think something that would have
helped me was to do a little bit more

00:18:20.930 --> 00:18:24.740
introspection and figure out my focus and
goals before I came here.

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:28.770
I had this general idea that I wanted
a reason or a mission to get up in

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:32.400
the morning and to go to work, and
that was doing good in the world.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:33.750
But I had no idea what that meant,

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:38.500
and I think a tangible example of that for
me is I was applying for our Impact Fund.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:41.325
And we do investment deals
in a variety of areas,

00:18:41.325 --> 00:18:45.970
in financial technology,
health care, education, energy and

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:50.620
environment, urban development,
kind all over the place.

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:53.520
And they asked us to choose
what we were interested in, and

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:56.750
I knew that I was interested in investing
in things that were good for the world.

00:18:56.750 --> 00:19:00.917
But I had no idea which of those
spaces I was actually interested in.

00:19:00.917 --> 00:19:05.252
And that meant that I was kind of all
over the place, and I didn't focus.

00:19:05.252 --> 00:19:08.740
Then once I was able to sort of
narrow in on what it was that I

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:12.230
truly cared the most about,
life got a lot easier for me,

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:16.420
it was a lot easier to say no to
things that weren't quite relevant.

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:20.171
I think there's always this element of,
well, everybody dropped their resume at

00:19:20.171 --> 00:19:23.080
Google, I should probably drop
my resume at Google, too.

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:26.546
And maybe that's true, but maybe it's not.

00:19:26.546 --> 00:19:29.912
And so just knowing some of these things
about yourself before you come here, and

00:19:29.912 --> 00:19:31.793
all of these shiny objects
are thrown at you,

00:19:31.793 --> 00:19:33.540
I think can be a really helpful exercise.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.540
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll go in a slightly
different direction for this one.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:43.199
I wish I had known more about the power
of, first, of Stanford network, but

00:19:43.199 --> 00:19:44.754
also the Stanford email.

00:19:44.754 --> 00:19:49.752
An example that I've given a few people
recently is that over the past months,

00:19:49.752 --> 00:19:55.090
I've emailed four hospital CEOs just to
ask them to sit down and chat with me.

00:19:55.090 --> 00:19:57.060
And all four them responded.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:20:00.580
Three out of the four
responded within 24 hours, and

00:20:00.580 --> 00:20:05.000
we set up little, small,
short calls of half an hour.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:09.050
And they were all very willing to tell me
about their experiences, about how they

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:14.550
got to where they got, and to give me
advice on how to get to where they are at.

00:20:14.550 --> 00:20:16.640
And so had I known about that earlier,

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:19.340
I think I would have taken much
more advantage of that last year,

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:22.825
even as soon as I got my Stanford
email when I first got it.

00:20:22.825 --> 00:20:26.895
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah, that makes sense.

00:20:26.895 --> 00:20:31.391
&gt;&gt; What has been the greatest
challenge you have faced at Stanford?

00:20:31.391 --> 00:20:33.989
&gt;&gt; Hm, do you want to start?

00:20:33.989 --> 00:20:34.788
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:20:34.788 --> 00:20:37.679
&gt;&gt; I'll take that one.

00:20:37.679 --> 00:20:39.941
I think two things.

00:20:39.941 --> 00:20:42.490
First, I'm an international student.

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:46.631
And so adapting to a new culture
in a new country has been

00:20:46.631 --> 00:20:49.430
more difficult than I anticipated.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:53.112
Especially because I come from Canada, so
I expected the culture to be very similar,

00:20:53.112 --> 00:20:54.640
but there are some differences.

00:20:54.640 --> 00:20:59.326
In terms of the politics, in terms of the
culture, the cultural references, and so

00:20:59.326 --> 00:21:03.286
it was kind of interesting for me to
realize that I was in a very different

00:21:03.286 --> 00:21:06.600
environment sort of mid way
through my fall quarter.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:09.130
And had I braced myself a bit more for
that,

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:12.300
I think my transition would
have been a bit smoother.

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.970
And the other thing is time management.

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:16.320
As Heather was mentioning, there's so

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:19.990
many opportunities that are thrown at you
here all the time that it's hard sometimes

00:21:19.990 --> 00:21:24.160
to decide which one is
the best use of your time.

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:27.087
&gt;&gt; I would definitely
echo time management.

00:21:27.087 --> 00:21:30.551
I think for me, the second dimension
is much more personal, and

00:21:30.551 --> 00:21:35.320
that's that my husband lives in Chicago,
and so we're in a long-distance marriage.

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:41.072
We got married two months before I moved
here to start my two-year MBA program.

00:21:41.072 --> 00:21:46.060
And that's hard, we're doing well, but
of course, we would rather be together.

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:50.789
So I think just figuring out, I think a
lot of people struggle with how to balance

00:21:50.789 --> 00:21:54.672
their relationships and
lives pre-GSP with the fact that this is

00:21:54.672 --> 00:21:58.841
an all-consuming experience that you
really want to be present for and

00:21:58.841 --> 00:22:02.189
take advantage of over the two
years that you're here.

00:22:02.189 --> 00:22:05.384
So finding that balance for yourself and
how to take care of yourself and

00:22:05.384 --> 00:22:07.470
your other relationships
outside of the GSP.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:14.550
&gt;&gt; What's the single most important area
Stanford has offered you to improve your

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:16.020
market value so far?

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:18.400
&gt;&gt; Ooh, my market value.

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:20.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a very interesting one.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.070
I think I have an idea, actually.

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:22.570
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:26.854
&gt;&gt; I would say it's made me more aware
of my market value, if anything.

00:22:26.854 --> 00:22:31.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think as someone who was more

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:36.060
an individual contributor,
I didn't realize how valuable

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:39.580
the skills that I already
possessed before the GSP were.

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:42.860
And I didn't realize that there were so
many different things that I could be

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:48.230
doing within and outside health care with
what I've learned both here and before.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:52.710
And so I think Stanford has
taught me to think big and

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:57.980
to know that if I work hard and if I'm
passionate about what I'm doing, I can

00:22:57.980 --> 00:23:01.750
accomplish a lot of things that I never
even thought were possible in the past.

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:04.820
&gt;&gt; I'm going to fork this
in two directions, I think.

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:11.380
First, what I think outside
perception of my market value is.

00:23:11.380 --> 00:23:15.400
And then where I actually think my
market value has improved the most from

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:18.830
an internal perspective, because I
think those are different things.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:23.400
From the outside perspective, honestly,
just the signal of a Stanford MBA

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:27.870
credential is what I think has
improved my market value the most.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:31.290
There are groups that I emailed
beforehand, as I consulted at

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:35.940
very good consulting firm prior to
the GSP, when I was kind of thinking about

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:39.720
different career options, when I was
debating between that and business school.

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:41.052
And I never got an email back.

00:23:41.052 --> 00:23:45.490
And then suddenly you are a Stanford MBA,
and everyone emails you back.

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:49.478
So just having the credential is
in of itself of market value, and

00:23:49.478 --> 00:23:53.625
that feels a little transactional to say,
but I think it's there.

00:23:53.625 --> 00:23:58.363
And then internally where I actually
think that I have gained the most market

00:23:58.363 --> 00:24:01.480
value is in the soft skills, absolutely.

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:07.000
I think that before when I was in really
high pressure consulting environments,

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:09.440
it was easy for me to be impatient or

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:14.170
speak sharply in a way that
could be demotivating to a team.

00:24:14.170 --> 00:24:19.073
And I really learned how to adapt sort of
a firm style with being a little bit more

00:24:19.073 --> 00:24:24.052
empathetic in ways that I think will
maybe they'll come across in interviews,

00:24:24.052 --> 00:24:25.778
maybe they'll be valued.

00:24:25.778 --> 00:24:29.690
But they will certainly be valued
when I'm managing teams or

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:34.215
leading teams and the fact that I feel
much more equipped to develop and

00:24:34.215 --> 00:24:36.760
grow a team to their utmost potential.

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:42.880
&gt;&gt; How collaborative is the GSB
environment do the second year students

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:46.430
help with recruiting preparation is
the classroom dynamics friendly?

00:24:47.750 --> 00:24:50.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah, classroom dynamics very friendly.

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:54.980
We have a student agreed upon
norm here a grade non disclosure,

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:58.780
which means that to recruiters, you
aren't allowed to share your class rank,

00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:00.580
you're not allowed to share your GPA.

00:25:01.870 --> 00:25:05.270
So it's super collaborative,
I think last year.

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:08.500
People were building certain guides and
sending them out

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:13.700
to the entire class saying hi, I can pile
all the slide, good luck on your finals.

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:16.804
So definitely collaborative and

00:25:16.804 --> 00:25:23.700
helpful if not competitive [LAUGH]
I don't think at all and then.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:25.190
What was the other half of the question?

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:27.585
&gt;&gt; Do second year students help
with recruiting preparation?

00:25:27.585 --> 00:25:31.494
&gt;&gt; Yeah, a large portion of our
classes interested in slightly more

00:25:31.494 --> 00:25:33.410
entrepreneurial ventures.

00:25:33.410 --> 00:25:36.700
I think where I see the most help
in career preparation is that

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:40.020
if there's somebody that's in the field
that you want to go into, they will

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:44.480
if you email them and they're like, hey,
can we sit down for 30 minutes for coffee?

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:46.313
They will tell you about
their summer experience.

00:25:46.313 --> 00:25:49.570
We do a ton of panels on like,
what did I do last summer?

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:51.060
How did I go through that process?

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:52.219
What did it look like?

00:25:52.219 --> 00:25:55.183
And then there are a few things that
are a little bit more institutionalized.

00:25:55.183 --> 00:25:59.700
And so, and for consulting for example,
I ran cases with a lot of people last year

00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:02.890
to help prepare them for
their case interviews.

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:05.850
And maybe you can speak more
about that from your perspective.

00:26:05.850 --> 00:26:07.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was recruiting for

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:11.580
consulting and obviously we have a lot
of classmates who work in consulting.

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.925
We have a consulting club, and so,

00:26:13.925 --> 00:26:19.255
I lean into both of those resources pretty
heavily to prepare for my interviews.

00:26:19.255 --> 00:26:23.452
And that, honestly that made the
difference between me getting the job or

00:26:23.452 --> 00:26:25.110
not at the end of the day.

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:28.200
So, I think it's incredibly collaborative.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:33.460
The other thing I'll say about
that is that we all become kind of

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:37.910
known to each other because the class size
is small enough that you have at least

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:42.240
an idea of what people are interested in,
what they're like a little bit.

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:45.350
And so
whenever I hear of an opportunity and

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:50.700
social impact or in fashion, for example,
I'll think of Heather immediately.

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:53.130
And if she hears of
something within healthcare,

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:56.290
like healthcare systems policy
like she'll think of me.

00:26:56.290 --> 00:26:59.840
And so opportunities kind of
get redirected a lot like that

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:03.180
which is super helpful for
us to achieve our goals and

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:06.150
learn about things that we wouldn't
have heard about otherwise.

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:08.880
&gt;&gt; I think people are super generous
with their networks here and

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:12.180
pass along job opportunities that
might be of interest all the time.

00:27:12.180 --> 00:27:14.400
And if there's something
that you mentioned,

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:16.150
they'll be like I have
a friend that does that.

00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:17.250
Let me introduce you.

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:18.355
So I think that's been.

00:27:18.355 --> 00:27:19.375
That are super helpful.

00:27:19.375 --> 00:27:20.125
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely.

00:27:22.245 --> 00:27:24.605
How was your experience with
the application process, and

00:27:24.605 --> 00:27:27.495
what is your piece of advice
to potential applicants?

00:27:27.495 --> 00:27:30.205
&gt;&gt; Great, and I think this will
probably have to be our last question.

00:27:30.205 --> 00:27:30.705
&gt;&gt; I think so.

00:27:32.035 --> 00:27:33.665
&gt;&gt; So the experience with
the application question,

00:27:33.665 --> 00:27:38.475
I think Stanford was by far the most
involved application that I completed.

00:27:38.475 --> 00:27:43.910
So I would say open it up and
look through it entirely.

00:27:43.910 --> 00:27:47.540
Well in advance of the deadline if you if
you know the essay prompts and you think

00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:50.950
you'll just do that and filling your name
and address, there's more to it than that.

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:52.970
So look in advance.

00:27:52.970 --> 00:27:56.830
And but
overall the application process was great.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:28:00.950
I think my advice for
you is well, first of all,

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:04.680
again this is a black box, none of us
knows exactly why we were admitted.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:09.101
So we don't say anything on
behalf of the admissions office.

00:28:09.101 --> 00:28:13.036
But for me I think it was really
important to me that I show that I

00:28:13.036 --> 00:28:15.989
was a well rounded person
who had interests and

00:28:15.989 --> 00:28:20.600
passions and just things that
I cared about outside of work.

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:24.670
And if you have turned your passion
into your work, that is great,

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:28.068
talk about your work by all means but,
all the facets of your personality.

00:28:28.068 --> 00:28:28.955
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:28:28.955 --> 00:28:33.910
I think the only thing I'll add to that is
just try to be as authentic as you can.

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:36.950
None of us know what the admissions
office is looking for.

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:39.030
So you may as well be
your authentic self so

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:42.090
that you know that the person they
accepted is the person you actually

00:28:42.090 --> 00:28:46.110
are not a person that you've tried to
manufacture for an admissions committee.

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:52.740
I think they're very intentional
about creating a community and you

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:55.550
want to know that you're the person that
they wanted as part of this community.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:28:58.080
&gt;&gt; Self reflection is good.

00:28:58.080 --> 00:28:59.440
&gt;&gt; Definitely.

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:01.440
So, we're almost out
of time unfortunately.

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:02.650
But before we go Heather,

00:29:02.650 --> 00:29:05.920
do you have any final words to share with
the applicants or a piece or advice?

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:11.179
&gt;&gt; This is the greatest two years ever.

00:29:11.179 --> 00:29:16.201
So, just have fun, try not to stress
too much, the allocation process

00:29:16.201 --> 00:29:20.910
seems never ending, but
it's really worth it on the other side.

